tests/ui/check/ok_cost_report.sky:2:5: note: cost of `gram`: M²·N + M·N flops, 1 allocation of M² floats
  |
2 | def gram(A: Matrix[N, M]) -> Matrix[M, M]:
  |     ^^^^
  = note: memory traffic: 16·M·N bytes read, 8·M² bytes written

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_report.sky:3:12: note: [LA-TRANSPOSE-001] `Aᵀ @ A` is rewritten to `matmul[ta](A, A)`
  |
3 |     return Aᵀ @ A
  |            ^^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `transposes` pass
  = note: because the transposed operand is a matrix: `matmul[ta]` reads it transposed, so the N·M element copy that `transpose` makes is never made
  = note: because exact: `matmul[ta]` is defined as the transpose followed by the product, so every bit and every error is the same; a kernel may read the operand with swapped strides
  = note: because the flops are unchanged: 2·N·M²

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_report.sky:3:12: note: [LA-GRAM-001] `Aᵀ @ A` is rewritten to `matmul[ta,alg=syrk](A, A)`
  |
3 |     return Aᵀ @ A
  |            ^^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `structure` pass
  = note: because A is multiplied by its own transpose: entries (i, j) and (j, i) sum the same products (`aₚᵢ·aₚⱼ` and `aₚⱼ·aₚᵢ`, and multiplication commutes) in the same order
  = note: because one triangle is computed and mirrored: N·M² + N·M flops instead of 2·N·M²
  = note: because exact: the result is bit for bit the general product's, and bitwise symmetric (its type gains the Symmetric tag)

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_report.sky:5:5: note: cost of `step`: 2·N flops, 1 allocation of N floats
  |
5 | def step(v: Vector[N], w: Vector[N]) -> Vector[N]:
  |     ^^^^
  = note: memory traffic: 16·N bytes read, 8·N bytes written

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_report.sky:6:12: note: [FUSE-001] `v + 2 * w` is rewritten to `fused(2, w, v)`
  |
6 |     return v + 2 * w
  |            ^^^^^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `fuse` pass
  = note: because fused 2 elementwise operations into one kernel; saves 1 temporary array of shape Vector[N]
  = note: because every intermediate result is used exactly once, by the next operation, and every array operand is a Vector[N]
  = note: because 2 of the operations can fail (`mul`, `add`); the kernel reports the failure of the first of them in program order that fails at any element, which is the error the separate operations gave

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_report.sky:6:12: note: [PAR-001] this elementwise kernel runs on 8 threads once it has at least 2048 elements
  |
6 |     return v + 2 * w
  |            ^^^^^^^^^
  = note: because its size is known only when the program runs; below the threshold splitting costs more than it saves
  = note: because every element is independent, so the result is bit-identical to the sequential one whatever the split
  = note: because 2 of its operations can fail (`mul`, `add`): if chunks fail, the failing operation that comes first in program order is reported, at its lowest failing element, which is the error a sequential run gives

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_report.sky:8:5: note: cost of `relax`: N² flops, N allocations (N² floats in all)
  |
8 | def relax(v: Vector[N]) -> Vector[N]:
  |     ^^^^^
  = note: memory traffic: 8·N² bytes read, 8·N² bytes written

tests/ui/check/ok_cost_report.sky:10:13: note: [PAR-001] this elementwise `mul` runs on 8 threads once it has at least 262144 elements
   |
10 |         v = v * 2
   |             ^^^^^
   = note: because its size is known only when the program runs; below the threshold splitting costs more than it saves
   = note: because every element is independent, so the result is bit-identical to the sequential one whatever the split
   = note: because its only failure is an overflow (or a zero divisor) of the whole operation, found after the elements are computed and reported the same way whichever thread found it

